[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Router0 is healthy; the outage is not an egress or RIP failure on router0. Evidence: PC4 reaches other external networks (e.g., 192.168.200.12) and has a working default route via 172.16.10.1. Router0’s RIP is up and learning/advertising routes (show ip rip shows learned routes to 192.168.200.0/24, 172.18.50/51.0/24, etc.). Router0’s uplinks are up (eth2 10.16.1.1 and eth4 172.20.1.1) and it can ping 10.16.1.2 and 172.18.51.254 successfully.

The specific failure is toward Location C (172.18.51.0/24). Traceroute from PC4 to 172.18.51.15 reaches 10.50.1.2 (router6) then dies, indicating drops at router6. On router6, iptables shows FORWARD chain rules that drop all traffic arriving on eth2 (the core-facing 10.50.1.2) except a narrow TCP/80 exception to 172.18.51.1. The drop counters are incrementing, and router6 can ping local hosts (172.18.51.15) while hosts elsewhere cannot reach them—consistent with forwarding being blocked (INPUT is ACCEPT so pings to 172.18.51.254 succeed, but transit traffic is dropped).

Localization: router6, FORWARD chain on interface eth2 (10.50.1.2). Root cause: Misconfigured firewall (iptables) on router6 is dropping forwarded traffic from the core to Location C subnets. Corrective fix: On router6, remove or adjust the FORWARD rules to permit required inter-site traffic (e.g., iptables -F FORWARD or add explicit ACCEPTs for 172.16.10.0/24 → 172.18.51.0/24 and ICMP), then persist changes. No action is needed on router0’s egress or RIP. 
=== END ===